## Catalogue and exact lookup

The array, entries and colors are frozen to protect the shared snapshot. A Map
also makes prototype names such as `constructor` and `__proto__` ordinary misses.

```ts : <<Theme catalogue>>=
function luminance(hex: string): number {
    const channels = [1, 3, 5].map(offset => {
        const channel = parseInt(hex.slice(offset, offset + 2), 16) / 255;
        return channel <= 0.04045 ? channel / 12.92 : Math.pow((channel + 0.055) / 1.055, 2.4);
    });
    return channels[0] * 0.2126 + channels[1] * 0.7152 + channels[2] * 0.0722;
}

export const themes: readonly LiterateTheme[] = Object.freeze(paletteRows.map(row => {
    const [name, ...values] = row.split(' ');
    const colors = {} as LiterateTheme['colors'];
    colorKeys.forEach((key, index) => { colors[key] = values[index]; });
    return Object.freeze({ name, dark: luminance(colors.background) < luminance(colors.text),
        colors: Object.freeze(colors) });
}));

const themesByName = new Map(themes.map(theme => [theme.name, theme]));

export function getTheme(name: string): LiterateTheme | undefined {
    return themesByName.get(name);
}
```

## Shared syntax colors and format policy

These ordered groups are the single scope-to-palette source for document CSS and
publication syntax. Keeping `link` separate preserves the existing CSS order.
Only base scopes select colors: highlight.js emits `title.function` as classes
`hljs-title function_`, while publication receives the dotted scope directly.
Unknown scopes (including prototype names) reset to text. No runtime imports,
CSS parsing, palette adjustment or language dependency are needed here.

```ts : <<Theme syntax scope groups>>=
interface SyntaxColorGroup {
    scopes: string;
    color: keyof LiterateTheme['colors'];
}
const syntaxColorGroups: readonly SyntaxColorGroup[] = [
    { scopes: 'keyword selector-tag literal', color: 'purple' },
    { scopes: 'string regexp addition', color: 'accent' },
    { scopes: 'number symbol bullet', color: 'warning' },
    { scopes: 'title section built_in type', color: 'information' },
    { scopes: 'attr attribute variable template-variable', color: 'cyan' },
    { scopes: 'comment quote', color: 'muted' },
    { scopes: 'meta doctag', color: 'yellow' },
    { scopes: 'deletion name', color: 'danger' },
    { scopes: 'link', color: 'information' }
];
const syntaxColorKeys = new Map(syntaxColorGroups.flatMap(group =>
    group.scopes.split(' ').map(scope => [scope, group.color] as const)));
export function syntaxColor(scope: string, theme: LiterateTheme): string {
    return theme.colors[syntaxColorKeys.get(scope.split('.')[0]) ?? 'text'];
}
```

Colors are shared; typography remains a format adapter's responsibility.
Standalone HTML and the live preview both consume `themeCss` (the preview's
`applyTheme` installs it even without diagrams). Preserve screen comment/quote
italics, link underlines and the separate emphasis/strong rules. Do not add
publication's keyword/variable bold policy or reset host font inheritance here.
LaTeX deliberately supplies its own weights and never underlines syntax links.

```ts : <<Theme syntax adapters>>=
function syntaxColorCss(): string {
    return syntaxColorGroups.map(group => {
        const selectors = group.scopes.split(' ')
            .map(scope => `.literate-document .hljs-${scope}`).join(', ');
        const decoration = group.scopes === 'comment quote' ? ' font-style: italic;'
            : group.scopes === 'link' ? ' text-decoration: underline;' : '';
        return `${selectors} { color: var(--literate-${group.color});${decoration} }`;
    }).join('\n');
}
```

## Safe custom properties

Only fixed property names and six-digit RGB literals are serialized, even when a
caller supplies a custom structural `LiterateTheme`. Invalid colors throw rather
than becoming CSS, HTML or Mermaid configuration injection. Theme names are never
interpolated into output. Extra object properties are ignored.

```ts : <<Theme serialization>>=
function checkedColors(theme: LiterateTheme): LiterateTheme['colors'] {
    const colors = {} as LiterateTheme['colors'];
    for (const key of colorKeys) {
        const value = theme.colors[key];
        if (typeof value !== 'string' || value.length !== 7 || !/^#[0-9a-fA-F]{6}$/.test(value)) {
            throw new Error(`Invalid theme color: ${key}`);
        }
        colors[key] = value;
    }
    return colors;
}

export function themeVariables(theme: LiterateTheme): string {
    const colors = checkedColors(theme);
    return colorKeys.map(key => {
        const property = key.replace(/[A-Z]/g, letter => `-${letter.toLowerCase()}`);
        return `--literate-${property}: ${colors[key]};`;
    }).join('\n');
}
```

## Mermaid adapter

This is a configuration **patch** for `Mermaid.initialize`, not a replacement for
caller policy. It returns only `theme` and `themeVariables`; security level,
startup policy, resource limits and diagram options remain owned by callers.
Explicit flowchart, sequence, class/ER and pie variables keep diagram text and
surfaces coordinated with the document. Fonts are local system stacks.

```ts : <<Theme Mermaid configuration>>=
export function mermaidTheme(theme: LiterateTheme) {
    const c = checkedColors(theme);
    return {
        theme: 'base' as const,
        themeVariables: {
            <<Mermaid base and flowchart variables>>
            <<Mermaid sequence variables>>
            <<Mermaid class and relation variables>>
            <<Mermaid pie variables>>
        }
    };
}
```

### Base surfaces and flowcharts

Mermaid's base theme accepts explicit semantic colors. Establish typography and
three surface tiers first, then assign nodes, clusters and edge labels so a
flowchart does not fall back to colors from the editor host.

```ts : <<Mermaid base and flowchart variables>>=
darkMode: theme.dark === true,
background: c.background, textColor: c.text,
fontFamily: 'system-ui, -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, "Segoe UI", sans-serif',
fontSize: '16px',
primaryColor: c.surface, primaryTextColor: c.text, primaryBorderColor: c.accent,
secondaryColor: c.surfaceRaised, secondaryTextColor: c.text, secondaryBorderColor: c.information,
tertiaryColor: c.background, tertiaryTextColor: c.text, tertiaryBorderColor: c.border,
lineColor: c.information, mainBkg: c.surface, nodeBorder: c.accent,
nodeTextColor: c.text, clusterBkg: c.surfaceRaised, clusterBorder: c.border,
titleColor: c.text, edgeLabelBackground: c.background,
```

### Sequence diagrams

Actors and signals reuse the document's surface and information roles. Notes
receive warning borders, while activations use the accent; both retain raised
surfaces so their text follows the same foreground policy as other labels.

```ts : <<Mermaid sequence variables>>=
actorBkg: c.surface, actorBorder: c.accent, actorTextColor: c.text,
actorLineColor: c.border, signalColor: c.information, signalTextColor: c.text,
labelBoxBkgColor: c.surfaceRaised, labelBoxBorderColor: c.border,
labelTextColor: c.text, loopTextColor: c.text,
noteBkgColor: c.surfaceRaised, noteTextColor: c.text, noteBorderColor: c.warning,
activationBkgColor: c.surfaceRaised, activationBorderColor: c.accent,
sequenceNumberColor: c.background,
```

### Classes and relationships

Alternating attribute rows distinguish structure without changing text color.
Relationship labels sit on the document background, keeping their treatment
consistent with the lines that connect entities.

```ts : <<Mermaid class and relation variables>>=
classText: c.text, attributeBackgroundColorOdd: c.surface,
attributeBackgroundColorEven: c.surfaceRaised,
relationColor: c.information, relationLabelColor: c.text,
relationLabelBackground: c.background,
```

### Pie charts

The categorical sequence draws from the same accents rather than introducing a
second palette. Section labels and slice strokes use the background color,
whereas titles and legends retain the document foreground.

```ts : <<Mermaid pie variables>>=
pie1: c.accent, pie2: c.information, pie3: c.warning, pie4: c.danger,
pie5: c.purple, pie6: c.cyan, pie7: c.yellow, pie8: c.muted,
pie9: c.accent, pie10: c.information, pie11: c.purple, pie12: c.cyan,
pieTitleTextColor: c.text, pieSectionTextColor: c.background,
pieLegendTextColor: c.text, pieStrokeColor: c.background,
pieOuterStrokeColor: c.border
```

## Standalone document styles

Every selector is rooted at `.literate-document`, including responsive rules.
The wrapper owns its box, background and typography, without styling `body`,
`:root` or the editor host. Code and wide tables scroll locally; prose wraps.
SVG geometry and Mermaid's own text colors are left to its configuration.
Reset syntax-token foregrounds before applying palette-specific rules: VS Code's
host theme explicitly colors tokens such as parameters and functions, which
would otherwise leak a dark editor's pale text into a light document.

```ts : <<Theme document stylesheet>>=
export function themeCss(theme: LiterateTheme): string {
    return `<<Document CSS layout>>
<<Document CSS typography>>
<<Document CSS code>>
<<Document CSS tables>>
<<Document CSS diagrams and media>>
<<Document CSS responsive and license>>
`;
}
```

These are source fragments inside one template literal, not runtime string
joins. References stay at column zero so expansion introduces no indentation;
the newline between each pair of fragments is part of the exported CSS. Keep
literal opening fragment delimiters escaped according to the shared generator's
rules when adding examples to these strings.

### Layout and scope

The wrapper establishes a bounded reading column and owns the palette variables.
Its local box-sizing reset makes nested content predictable without imposing
layout or a color scheme on the surrounding editor page.

```css : <<Document CSS layout>>=
.literate-document {
${themeVariables(theme)}
    display: block;
    box-sizing: border-box;
    color-scheme: ${theme.dark === true ? 'dark' : 'light'};
    width: 100%;
    max-width: 80rem;
    min-width: 0;
    margin: 0 auto;
    padding: clamp(1rem, 4vw, 3rem);
    background: var(--literate-background);
    color: var(--literate-text);
    font: 16px/1.65 system-ui, -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, "Segoe UI", sans-serif;
    overflow-wrap: anywhere;
}
.literate-document *, .literate-document *::before, .literate-document *::after { box-sizing: border-box; }
```

### Reading typography

Headings establish hierarchy; lists and definitions retain their native reading
structure. Links keep underlines and visible keyboard focus, while quotations
use a surface and accent border rather than relying on italics alone.

```css : <<Document CSS typography>>=
.literate-document h1, .literate-document h2, .literate-document h3,
.literate-document h4, .literate-document h5, .literate-document h6 {
    color: var(--literate-text); line-height: 1.25; margin: 1.5em 0 .65em; font-weight: 650;
}
.literate-document h1 { font-size: 2.25em; }
.literate-document h2 { font-size: 1.75em; border-bottom: 1px solid var(--literate-border); padding-bottom: .3em; }
.literate-document h3 { font-size: 1.4em; }
.literate-document h4 { font-size: 1.15em; }
.literate-document h5, .literate-document h6 { font-size: 1em; }
.literate-document p, .literate-document ul, .literate-document ol, .literate-document dl { margin: 0 0 1em; }
.literate-document ul { list-style: disc; padding-left: 1.75em; }
.literate-document ol { list-style: decimal; padding-left: 1.75em; }
.literate-document li { margin: .25em 0; }
.literate-document li::before { content: none; }
.literate-document dt { font-weight: 600; }
.literate-document dd { margin: 0 0 .75em 1.5em; }
.literate-document a { color: var(--literate-information); text-decoration: underline; text-underline-offset: .15em; }
.literate-document a:hover { color: var(--literate-accent); }
.literate-document a:focus-visible { outline: 2px solid var(--literate-accent); outline-offset: 3px; }
.literate-document strong { font-weight: 700; }
.literate-document em { font-style: italic; }
.literate-document hr { border: 0; border-top: 1px solid var(--literate-border); margin: 2em 0; }
.literate-document blockquote { margin: 1em 0; padding: .75em 1em; border-left: .25em solid var(--literate-accent); background: var(--literate-surface); }
```

### Code and literate fragments

Monospace text and fragment panels share the same surface treatment. Preformatted
blocks scroll instead of wrapping; fragment names can wrap to avoid widening the
page. A fragment panel owns its border; its nested preformatted block stays
borderless so the name and implementation read as one unit. Standalone code
blocks retain their own border. The token reset must precede the semantic highlighting rules so inherited
host colors cannot override the chosen palette's baseline foreground.

```css : <<Document CSS code>>=
.literate-document pre, .literate-document code, .literate-document kbd, .literate-document samp,
.literate-document .fragmentname { font-family: ui-monospace, SFMono-Regular, Consolas, monospace; }
.literate-document code, .literate-document kbd, .literate-document samp { background: var(--literate-surface); color: var(--literate-text); padding: .15em .3em; border-radius: .2em; font-size: .9em; }
.literate-document pre { background: var(--literate-surface); color: var(--literate-text); padding: 1em; margin: 1em 0; border: 1px solid var(--literate-border); border-radius: .35em; overflow: auto; white-space: pre; overflow-wrap: normal; line-height: 1.5; }
.literate-document pre code { padding: 0; border: 0; background: transparent; font-size: inherit; }
.literate-document .codefragment { margin: 1.5em 0; padding: 1em; border: 1px solid var(--literate-border); border-radius: .35em; background: var(--literate-surface); color: var(--literate-text); }
.literate-document .codefragment pre { border: 0; }
.literate-document .fragmentname { color: var(--literate-accent); overflow-wrap: anywhere; }
.literate-document .fragmentuse, .literate-document .literate-tag-name { color: var(--literate-purple); font-style: italic; }
.literate-document .code, .literate-document code div { color: var(--literate-text); }
.literate-document .code pre { margin: .5em 0; }
.literate-document .codefragment > div + div { margin-top: .75em; }
.literate-document [class*="hljs-"] { color: var(--literate-text); background: transparent; }
.literate-document .hljs, .literate-document .hljs-subst { color: var(--literate-text); background: var(--literate-surface); }
${syntaxColorCss()}
.literate-document .hljs-emphasis { font-style: italic; }
.literate-document .hljs-strong { font-weight: bold; }
```

### Tables

Wide tables own their horizontal scrolling rather than stretching the reading
column. Tabular numerals aid comparison, and headers and alternating rows use
the two surface levels already established for structured content.

```css : <<Document CSS tables>>=
.literate-document table { display: block; max-width: 100%; overflow: auto; border-collapse: collapse; margin: 1em 0; font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums; }
.literate-document th, .literate-document td { border: 1px solid var(--literate-border); padding: .5em .8em; text-align: left; }
.literate-document th { background: var(--literate-surface-raised); font-weight: 650; }
.literate-document tr:nth-child(even) { background: var(--literate-surface); }
.literate-document caption { color: var(--literate-muted); padding: .5em; text-align: left; }
```

### Media and diagrams

Media scale within the column while preserving aspect ratio. Mermaid containers
can scroll, but these rules do not recolor SVG internals: that belongs to the
configuration patch above. A failed diagram is readable as an alert instead of
being centered like a successful rendering.

```css : <<Document CSS diagrams and media>>=
.literate-document img, .literate-document video { max-width: 100%; height: auto; }
.literate-document figure { margin: 1.5em 0; }
.literate-document figcaption { color: var(--literate-muted); }
.literate-document .mermaid, .literate-document .literate-mermaid { display: block; overflow: auto; padding: 1em; background: var(--literate-background); color: var(--literate-text); text-align: center; }
.literate-document .mermaid svg, .literate-document .literate-mermaid svg { display: block; max-width: 100%; height: auto; margin: auto; }
.literate-document .literate-mermaid[role="alert"] { color: var(--literate-danger); text-align: left; white-space: pre-wrap; }
```

### Narrow screens and retained license

Small screens reduce outer and fragment padding without changing document
structure. The retained comment ends the stylesheet, carrying the complete
upstream notice into every standalone export; its interpolation and trailing
newline remain inside the original template literal.

```css : <<Document CSS responsive and license>>=
@media (max-width: 40rem) {
    .literate-document { padding: 1rem; }
    .literate-document h1 { font-size: 1.8em; }
    .literate-document .codefragment { padding: .5em; }
}
/*!
${upstreamThemeNotice}
*/
```
